WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.700
Good morning, John. I come to you today in a series of ones and zeroes

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.700
passed through tubes made of copper and glass

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.000
retrieved instantaneously whenever you want to see it,

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.620
in fact, wherever you want to see it in the entire world.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:14.900
In an age of such wonders, I cannot help but ask the obvious question

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:16.920
'Why are we still doing television at all?'

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:18.700
I did it just the other day, sat down in front of a television -

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:21.740
the last time I had cable inside of my house was 2002 -

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:23.700
so all of the small little changes that have happened in those ten years

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:27.860
sort of come rushing on to me all at once and I have to say, it freaking sucks!

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:29.700
One of the joys of television used to be flipping channels.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.060
Now, this is no longer possible because something in the last ten years

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.540
has necessitated a five-second gap between when I push the up button

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:38.740
[five-second gap]

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:39.960
and when the next channel comes up.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.220
But flipping isn't supposed to be a thing anymore because

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:43.400
we have this wonderful thing called 'The Guide'.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.760
And if you could find the buttons on this monolithic remote

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:47.600
that allow you to access and interact with

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.140
the guide, good on you but once you do,

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:53.820
not only was I forced to scroll over one advertisement for every four channels I saw,

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.780
but huge expanses of the guide were not accessible to me.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:58.980
There were channels that I could not see. WHY ARE THEY IN MY GUIDE?

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.700
This should be a fairly simple technical challenge to overcome.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:03.840
I want to see the channels that I can see.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.000
And then I realize, "Hey, this is NBC. Channel seven-hundred AND TWELVE?!"

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:14.360
NO! NBC is supposed to be, like, 4 or 5 or 6. It's NBC. Why is it 712?

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:18.820
So I asked my friend, Dave, this and he's like 'Oh, NBC is lower down as well.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:23.480
When you upgrade to high definition you get high definition and standard definition NBC.'

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:27.040
WHY DO I WANT THE SD-NBC IF I HAVE THE HD ONE?!

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.600
And the HD one, the one that you want to watch, is in the 700s.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.420
I'm not gonna talk a lot about the content of television

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:33.820
because YouTube has crap too, you know?

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:35.640
But I'm a little bit frustrated by the advertisements running on

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.160
my science channel right now for a show called 'Finding Big Foot'.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:43.480
Ad: "I do think there's a 'squatch in these woods... FINDING BIGFOOT"

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:48.300
On Animal Planet? Which is supposed to be about animals. Not fake anim- well, I guess

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:52.140
insane people wandering around in forests are technically animals too.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.880
When I finally find something that I would like to watch

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.080
it's invariably in the wrong aspect ratio

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:57.300
because there is good television out there,

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.980
there's plenty of television that I would like to watch.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:05.000
Unfortunately, it's on, like 7 of the 130 channels that they give you.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.800
And four of those channels aren't even cable!

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:11.440
I could get them with antennas so that's three channels that I'm paying $130 a month for.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.780
That's why I can pay $8 for Netflix, get some of the best television of all time

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:17.680
and never have to watch a single advertisement because I'm freaking paying for it!

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.540
Oh, what's that I hear? You have to pay for cable and you still get advertisements? Great.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:25.360
An average hour of television, about 36% advertisements

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:27.780
if you include product placements, which means,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.680
if the average person in America watches 5 hours of television a day,

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:33.000
which apparently they do, that means that they will,

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.280
in their lives spend 4.5 years watching advertisements.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:39.760
I have done the math, my friends. Here in the world of YouTube,

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:43.160
a 15-second preroll on a 4-minute long video

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:47.120
is 7 times less advertising than you get on television.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:49.800
Not only that, I think that in general, online video is more engaging,

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.280
it's more likely to be what you want, it's more convenient.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:55.600
It's a much better interface, it's easier to discover

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:58.000
cool new stuff and I think it's more rewarding.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.500
So let me be possibly the first and hopefully not the last person to say:

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:06.440
"Everyone, cancel your cable subscriptions! It will be okay."

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:10.600
John and I are doing our part with a total of at least 6 videos on YouTube per week

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:15.180
if you subscribe to vlogbrothers, SciShow and CrashCourse, which will launch January 25th,

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.660
and you can more than double that number if you subscribe to hankgames

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:21.660
and Truth or Fail as well and I don't see why you wouldn't.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.960
Thank you to the world of online video and for you,

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:29.880
the pioneering viewers who are making this transition not just possible but necessary.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:31.860
John, I will see you on Tuesday.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:32.860
That sounds so weird.

